      {
        "type": "heading",
        "children": [
          {
            "text": "How to interpret the result"
          }
        ],
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
          {
            "text": "An improvement should retain or increase completeness at comparable MER agreement. A shift in score calibration can change the fixed-threshold plots without improving the tradeoff. MER agreement is not absolute object purity: real sources absent from MER count as unmatched. Counts are tile-pooled and matching is nearest-neighbour within 0.5 arcsec. VIS here means clean VIS sources with magnitude <24.5; NIR-only and full-MER completeness have no magnitude cut. These are development metrics, not a blind final test."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "children": [
          {
            "text": "Optimization diagnostics"
          }
        ],
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "children": [
          {
            "text": "The unknown arm omits some background penalties, so its loss can be lower by construction. Use these secondary plots to spot instability, not to decide which detector is better."
          }
        ]
      },
